# LEGACY-RME-PDF-ROLL-1 — Feature flag runtime override contract

1. **One registry, one resolver.** Every feature flag is defined in
   `config/feature_flags.php` and resolved through
   `App\Services\Foundation\FeatureFlagService`. Never build a second flag
   system, and never resolve a flag with a dotted key through
   `config('feature_flags.flags.<a>.<b>')` — the dots are literal array keys,
   so dot-notation traversal silently returns `null`.

2. **A declared `env_key` is a promise; capture is what keeps it.** An override
   only works if its value is captured into the config array while the config
   file is being built. Once a deployment runs `config:cache`, Laravel stops
   loading the environment file entirely, so a runtime `env()` read returns
   nothing and the declared default silently wins. `config/feature_flags.php`
   therefore captures `env_value` for every flag that declares an `env_key`,
   systematically, at the bottom of the file.

3. **The gap cannot come back.** `FeatureFlagService::validateGovernance()`
   FAILS (`FLAG-ENV-CAPTURE`) when any flag declares an `env_key` without a
   captured `env_value`. A new flag that forgets capture fails the registry,
   `foundation:feature-flags`, and the release gates.

4. **Resolution order.** Captured `env_value` → runtime `env()` (uncached
   environments only) → declared default.

5. **Fail closed, in both directions.** An unset *or blank* override means "not
   configured" and yields the declared default — blank is deliberately not read
   as `false`, so a default-true safety flag keeps its gate ON. An unparseable
   override also yields the declared default, is reported as `invalid`, and
   FAILS governance (`FLAG-ENV-VALUE-VALID`). A typo can never switch a risky
   default-off capability on.

6. **Never echo the raw override.** Evidence exposes a normalized
   `env_value` (`true` / `false` / `null` / the literal `'invalid'`), never the
   raw environment string.

7. **Server-side only.** No request parameter, query string, header, session,
   user preference or browser state may influence a flag. Flags come from
   trusted server configuration and nothing else.

8. **Risky flags default OFF.** Any high/critical flag must declare
   `default => false`. Enabling one is a WATCH (a deliberate, visible posture),
   never a silent GO.

9. **Rollback is deterministic and non-destructive.** Setting the override to
   `false` and rebuilding the config cache turns the capability off. It never
   requires a schema rollback and never deletes data.

10. **Code readiness ≠ production rollout.** A GO tag means the capability is
    implemented, gated and safe to *ship disabled*. Turning a flag on in
    production is a separate, explicit rollout decision. `rollout_status:
    implemented` describes the code, not the production switch.

11. **Legacy RME.** The archive runtime is reached only through
    `App\Modules\LegacyRme\Support\LegacyRmeFeatureGuard`, which delegates to
    `FeatureFlagService`. It stays OFF in production; while it is off every
    legacy route answers 404 server-side.